<Layout title="Reader privacy | ATHENA" description="How ATHENA reader accounts, reading preferences and newsletter subscriptions work."><article class="text-page"><p class="eyebrow">YOUR ATHENA</p><h1>Reader privacy</h1><h2>Reading the news</h2><p>You can read ATHENA’s public articles without creating a reader account. Our hosting services process requests needed to deliver the website.</p><h2>Your reader account</h2><p>Reader accounts use Supabase for authentication and storage. An account stores your email address, preferred news sections and the stories you choose to mark as read. Your preferences and reading history are restricted to your account.</p><h2>Restoring an earlier account</h2><p>To restore migrated preferences and reading history, you need a verified email address and the recovery code from your earlier ATHENA account. Keep that code private. If you do not have it, you can use a new reader account.</p><h2>Newsletter subscriptions</h2><p>ATHENA’s newsletter signup form is hosted by Substack. Opening or using it sends requests directly to Substack. Newsletter subscriptions are separate from reader accounts; manage them through Substack and use its unsubscribe options. <a href="https://substack.com/privacy">Read Substack’s privacy policy</a>.</p><h2>Account deletion</h2><p>When reader accounts are available, you can permanently delete your account, preferences and reading history from the account page. Deleting an ATHENA reader account does not unsubscribe you from Substack.</p><h2>Questions</h2><p><a href="/contact">Contact ATHENA</a> with reader privacy questions.</p></article></Layout>
